# Electricity and Magnetism: Charges, Currents, and Fields

> Static charge, flowing current, and magnetic fields are one
> story. This lesson runs from Coulomb to Ohm to Faraday, ending
> in the circuits wired through your home.

## 1. Electric Charge and Static Electricity

**Electric charge** comes in two kinds; like charges repel and
unlike charges attract. Charge can neither be created nor
destroyed — only transferred, as when rubbing a comb through dry
hair. Coulomb's law says the force between two charges grows with
the product of their sizes and falls with the square of their
distance.

A **lightning conductor** — Benjamin Franklin's idea — places a
pointed metal spike above a building so the giant spark chooses
the safe copper path to the ground instead of the walls.

## 2. Current and Potential Difference

@math label="eq:current"
I = \frac{Q}{t}, \qquad V = \frac{W}{Q}
@/math

**Electric current** is the rate of flow of charge, measured in
amperes; the **potential difference** between two points is the
work done per unit charge moved between them, measured in volts.

[example]
If 12 C of charge flows through a lamp in 4 s, the current is
I = 12 ÷ 4 = 3 A.
[/example]

## 3. Ohm's Law and Resistance

@math label="eq:ohms-law"
V = IR
@/math

At constant temperature, current grows in proportion to the
applied voltage; the constant of proportionality is the
**resistance**, measured in ohms (Ω).

| Factor | Effect on resistance |
| --- | --- |
| Longer wire | increases (R ∝ length) |
| Thicker wire | decreases (R ∝ 1/area) |
| Material | set by resistivity — copper low, nichrome high |
| Higher temperature | increases in metals |

[example]
A 12 V battery drives current through a 4 Ω lamp:
I = V/R = 12 ÷ 4 = 3 A.
[/example]

## 4. Series and Parallel Circuits

| Aspect | Series | Parallel |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Resistance | Rs = R₁ + R₂ | 1/Rp = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ |
| Current | same everywhere | divides between branches |
| Voltage | shared across components | same across each branch |
| One bulb fails | whole chain goes dark | others keep glowing |
| Example | fairy lights | household wiring |

[example]
Two 6 Ω resistors: in series Rs = 6 + 6 = 12 Ω; in parallel
1/Rp = 1/6 + 1/6 = 1/3, so Rp = 3 Ω — parallel lowers the total.
[/example]

## 5. Electrical Power and the Heating Effect

@math label="eq:power"
P = VI = I^{2}R = \frac{V^{2}}{R}
@/math

Power is measured in **watts**. Current passing through a
resistance heats it (Joule's law: H = I²Rt) — the working
principle of geysers, irons, and toasters. A **fuse** melts when
the current exceeds its rating, cutting the circuit and saving
appliances; an electric bulb glows because its thin **tungsten**
filament (melting point ≈ 3400 °C) white-hot resists melting.

@diagram tree id="current-effects" layout="tree" aria="Three effects of electric current with their applications"
  "Effects of current" -> "Heating: geyser, fuse, bulb"
  "Effects of current" -> "Magnetic: electromagnet, motor"
  "Effects of current" -> "Chemical: electroplating, electrolysis"
@/diagram

## 6. Magnetic Effects of Current

Hans Christian Oersted (1820) saw a compass needle swivel beside
a live wire — every current creates a magnetic field. The
**right-hand thumb rule** points the thumb along the current and
the curled fingers along the field circles. A coil wound as a
**solenoid** behaves like a bar magnet; with a soft-iron core it
becomes an **electromagnet**, switched on and off at will.

| Property | Electromagnet | Permanent magnet |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Strength | variable with current | fixed |
| On/off | temporary | always on |
| Poles | reversible by current direction | fixed |
| Uses | cranes, relays, doorbells, MRI | fridge magnets, compasses |

## 7. Electromagnetic Induction: Generator and Motor

Michael Faraday (1831) found the reverse of Oersted: moving a
magnet near a coil **induces** a current. A **generator**
(dynamo) uses this to convert mechanical energy into electrical
energy — every power plant is a generator turned by steam or
falling water. An **electric motor** runs the conversion the other
way, turning electrical energy into rotation through the force on
a current-carrying coil in a magnetic field.

## 8. Domestic Electric Circuits

Houses receive supply through a **live** (red/brown), a
**neutral** (black/blue), and an **earth** wire (green) — the
earth bonds metal bodies of appliances to the ground so a fault
cannot make the body live. Lighting and fans run on **5 A**
circuits; heavy appliances like geysers and air conditioners need
**15 A** lines. A **short circuit** happens when live and neutral
touch with almost no resistance; **overloading** is drawing more
current than the line is rated for — fuses and MCBs guard against
both.

## 9. Quick Revision

| Item | Remember |
| --- | --- |
| Current | I = Q/t, unit ampere |
| Ohm's law | V = IR at constant temperature |
| Series / parallel | Rs = R₁ + R₂; 1/Rp = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ |
| Power | P = VI = I²R = V²/R |
| Fuse | melts above its current rating |
| Oersted 1820 | a current deflects a compass — magnetism |
| Faraday 1831 | motion near a coil induces current — generator |
| Earth wire | green; bonds appliance bodies to ground |
